Thank you, John, for a kind introduction.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.359
And good morning, everybody.

00:02:22.359 --> 00:02:31.590
I was asked to talk on [? algorithms ?] in
data science to people who probably never

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:38.230
took an introductory algorithmic course and
who probably do not necessarily know how to

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:39.440
[INAUDIBLE].

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.530
It's a tall order, but I will try.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:49.769
And I want to focus in my talk on this intersection
of two worlds.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:57.670
One world is a world of biology, where problems
are somewhat imprecisely formulated; and the

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:03.840
world of [? algorithms ?] when w you absolutely
need to be precise in everything that you

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:04.989
are doing.

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:11.859
And I want to describe this breach with an
[INAUDIBLE] researcher and computational researcher.

00:03:11.859 --> 00:03:18.080
When it comes to problem formulation, when
a problem moves from biological world to algorithmic

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.930
world, what is happening?

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:25.620
In fact, many people underestimate the complexity
of this task.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:33.409
It takes enormous amount of time for a professional
bioinformatician to transform a problem from

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:38.139
imprecise biological formulation into an algorithmic
problem.

00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:39.739
Let's start.

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:47.040
Let's start from something that has little
to do with this biology-- the Newspaper Problem.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.769
Imagine that you have 100s of identical copies
of New York Times.

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:57.879
And you stack it on a pile of dynamite, and
you light the fuse.

00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:00.409
Of course, don't do it at home.

00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:02.510
And then there is an explosion.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:08.069
And after this explosion, many pieces are
burned, but something is left.

00:04:08.069 --> 00:04:15.309
And your goal is to reconstruct the original
New York Times newspaper.

00:04:15.309 --> 00:04:16.729
So what would you do?

00:04:16.729 --> 00:04:24.620
Well, you would look at tiny pieces and treat
them as a giant, overlapping puzzle.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:31.879
For example, when you look at these two pieces,
well, probably they come from the same place.

00:04:31.879 --> 00:04:34.800
And you can start assembling them together.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.990
And slowly but surely, the hope is that we
will eventually assemble the volumes.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:47.449
And you already have figured out that I am
actually talking about genome assembly in

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:49.389
disguise.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:58.789
And my goal would be to assemble the New York
Times front page [INAUDIBLE] a sequence in

00:04:58.789 --> 00:05:03.350
[INAUDIBLE] draft human genome, 17 years ago.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:07.759
And since that time, there was enormous acceleration
in genomic sequencing.

00:05:07.759 --> 00:05:15.710
And currently, there are only, if you took
a bacterial world, there are over 100,000

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.900
different bacteria sequence.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:25.110
And there are also huge efforts on sequencing
individual human genomes, of course.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:32.009
And our newspaper problem, obviously, it translated
in a biological problem, where instead of

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:36.909
New York Times, we have multiplied [? carcass
?] of a journal.

00:05:36.909 --> 00:05:39.490
And we break [? general ?] trend and position.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:42.069
We generate reads.

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:48.460
And of course, some, not all, reads from all
positions of the genome are present.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:53.340
And as a result, we have this huge collection
of reads.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:06:00.389
And for every read, we have no clue what position
in [? the journal ?] it come from.

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:07.099
Our goal, nevertheless, is to assemble these
pieces into a genome.

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:11.910
And we start looking for overlap exactly like
in the newspaper problem.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.860
Look, we just found an overlap of two pieces.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:20.810
Maybe we can glue them together and continue
in this way.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:22.400
That sounds simple.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.120
The only problem is that we have millions
or, in the case of human genome, billions

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.870
of such pieces.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:35.531
And any reasonable algorithm that you use
to assemble a jigsaw puzzle, even if you are

00:06:35.531 --> 00:06:41.210
an expert in assembling, let's say, sounds
and business [? pauses, ?] we'll fail, even

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.949
with the most powerful computer in the world.

00:06:43.949 --> 00:06:46.289
What will you do?

00:06:46.289 --> 00:06:53.139
So once again, our setup, we have experimental
challenge.

00:06:53.139 --> 00:06:54.749
But we solve it.

00:06:54.749 --> 00:06:56.749
We generate its reads.

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:59.180
And finally, we need to assemble it.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:03.879
How would we formulate this assembly problem?

00:07:03.879 --> 00:07:09.990
And I want you to meet these people who seemingly
have nothing to do with more than biology.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:15.930
At least, Euler and Hamilton have nothing
to do, because [? they were ?] born well before

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.090
modern biology was developed.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:24.120
But without them, we wouldn't be able to sequence
genomes.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:32.550
They came up with these fundamental mathematical
and algorithmic ideas that actually enable

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.199
genome sequencing today.

00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:40.970
And I will try to explain how their ideas
propagated into modern biology.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:48.529
As a result, in fact, today, many biologists,
for example, knows the term [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:48.529 --> 00:07:54.809
And currently, many biologists are working
[INAUDIBLE] this so-called reference genomes.

00:07:54.809 --> 00:08:00.960
In the future, the reference genomes, probably
they'll become an obsolete notion, because

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.189
they need to grow [INAUDIBLE] multiply genomes.

00:08:03.189 --> 00:08:06.059
And human genomes are very different.

00:08:06.059 --> 00:08:14.879
And they need to somehow represent this variety
of genome as something that people know, called

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:17.370
population graph,or population genome.

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:23.599
And this graph will be represented and is
currently represented based on the idea developed

00:08:23.599 --> 00:08:28.979
by De Bruijn, and going back, actually, to
Euler and Hamilton.

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:34.330
So let's see how would we solve the String
Reconstruction Problem.

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:42.640
And in our case, in the genome sequencing
problem setup, we need first to rigorously

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.240
formulate our [INAUDIBLE] problem.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:45.290
That's what I've done.

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:51.139
Our goal is to reconstruct the genome from
reads input to this problem, a collection

00:08:51.139 --> 00:08:53.970
of strings that we call reads.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:57.191
And output is a string genome reconstructed
from reads.

00:08:57.191 --> 00:08:59.540
Does it make sense?

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:07.670
Does it look like a rigorous statement that
you can pass to your algorithmic [? conic?

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:11.630
?] And this person will solve the problem.

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:19.029
This seemingly looking reasonable [INAUDIBLE]
formulation is absolutely wrong.

00:09:19.029 --> 00:09:21.700
This is not a computational problem.

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:31.900
But many biologists, in faced with a problem
like this, would view it as something that

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.750
represent their challenges.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:39.439
And maybe if [? exactly ?] in this way, if
they would work with algorithmic [? conics,

00:09:39.439 --> 00:09:45.060
?] they would represent their problem exactly
like I've done, without understanding that

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:49.060
it is not a computational problem that your
[? conic ?] [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:55.120
Let me explain why it is not a computational
problem.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:00.920
Sometime ago, genius mathematician Georg Cantor
said, "To ask the right question is harder

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:02.370
than to answer it."

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:07.440
And so the question above on this slide is
not the right question to ask.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:13.220
And when we think about problem formulation
and biological scientists, it's good to think

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:15.269
about this as a [INAUDIBLE] context.

00:10:15.269 --> 00:10:21.990
Imagine that you want to solve your biological
problems that require computation tools.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.840
But imagine that you never took [? algorithms
?] 101 class.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:28.800
And imagine that you don't even know how to
[INAUDIBLE].

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:35.010
Not to worry, you can enter into a million
dollar contract which this brilliant computer

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:39.890
scientist, Alice, to solve the genome sequencing
problem.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:41.882
But keep in mind that Alice lives in a bunker.

00:10:41.882 --> 00:10:48.149
And the only way you can communicate with
Alice is by passing her a single page.

00:10:48.149 --> 00:10:52.440
What is your problem formulation?

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.380
Our problem formulation on the previous slide
definitely fit on a single page.

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:58.450
So this will be satisfied.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.300
This requirement will be satisfied.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:07.529
Now, however, there are important rules for
communicating with Alice.

00:11:07.529 --> 00:11:09.880
You should avoid biological terms.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:12.790
Alice is very busy, and you are not her only
client.

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:18.500
She has no ability to ask you any question
or to know anything new about genomes.

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:20.560
She only knows computer science.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:25.240
As long as you formulate the problem, you
can be guaranteed she will solve it.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.259
But you need to formulate it correctly.

00:11:27.259 --> 00:11:31.380
If you pass Alice a page with terms like PCR,
she will fall

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:32.380
asleep.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:34.139
She doesn't understand.

00:11:34.139 --> 00:11:42.449
Now, the next bearing point on [INAUDIBLE],
your problem may have [? multiplied ?] algorithmically

00:11:42.449 --> 00:11:46.959
correct, but biologically useless solutions.

00:11:46.959 --> 00:11:51.190
And Alice actually is free to report any of
them, according to the contract.

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:56.110
She would prefer to report an easy-to-find
solution, because she's busy, and collect

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:59.610
your million dollar payment.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:05.610
It is important that you problem is formulated
in such a way that any solution written by

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:09.700
Alice will answer the question you are really
interested in.

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:17.660
It is not uncommon, when people formulate
problems, that allow to give useless, still

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:19.410
formally-correct solutions.

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:26.480
Another important thing, make sure that you
can check that Alice's solution is correct.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:32.170
A well-formulated problem specifies how to
determine whether the output is correct given

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.070
the input.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:37.620
If it does not, can you even check that Alice's
solution is correct?

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:41.790
In The problems that I formulated, this condition
was violated.

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:46.910
And as a result, it did not represent a good
computational problem.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:51.899
And of course, it is to say you have to be
specific, precise, short, elegant.

00:12:51.899 --> 00:13:00.810
Now, with this in mind, let's move toward
a good algorithmic problem formulation for

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:01.810
genome assembly.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:04.810
We will introduce the concept of k-mer composition.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:13.430
Given the genome k-mer composition, and k-mer
is simply a continuous substring of a genome

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:14.769
of [? lens ?] k.

00:13:14.769 --> 00:13:22.060
So [? 3-mer ?] composition will be simply
a collection of all [? 3mers ?] from the string.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:26.950
I'll take a liberty to represent it on a single
line.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:33.750
But the way I represent it here is a little
bit of chicken, because it assumes that I

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:38.450
know the order of this k-mer in my genome.

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:42.459
In reality, I don't know the order of the
k-mers.

00:13:42.459 --> 00:13:49.709
And that's why I will represent them in lexicographic
order, in the same way people represent information

00:13:49.709 --> 00:13:52.000
in a telephone book.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:56.560
So it's starting from all strings, starting
from A, and continuing [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:05.380
And then my string reconstruction problem
will be reconstruct a string from its k-mer

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:06.380
composition.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:13.829
Input is a collection of k-mers-- it means
your reads-- and output a genome, such that

00:14:13.829 --> 00:14:20.310
its composition is equal to the collection
of k-mers or your input trait.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:25.610
Of course, people working in assembly would
understand that it's a gross oversimplification

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:26.610
of reality.

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:33.000
But let's start from this, and then later,
we will move to a more adequate biological

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:34.110
problem formulation.

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:42.459
It's actually a very common approach in data
science to start from simplifying a problem

00:14:42.459 --> 00:14:50.509
to develop main ideas based on simplified
problem formulation and later move to problems

00:14:50.509 --> 00:14:54.350
that back a model biological reality.

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:58.370
Now, we now have the problem formulation.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:00.740
Let's start solving the problem.

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:08.310
Let's [? then ?] assume that I know which
particular [? 3-mer ?] represents a [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:14.790
Then if it is TAA is the first one, then the
next one should start from AA, right?

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:18.230
So let's bring the next one.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:19.300
And we're starting assembling.

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:23.170
So we bring the next one.

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:24.560
So here it is.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:25.560
Then bring the next one.

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:26.560
it is.

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:27.560
Then bring the next one.

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:31.160
But now we have three k-mers, starting from
TG.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:34.690
Not a problem, so simply, we have multiplied
choices.

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.839
Let's select one of them.

00:15:36.839 --> 00:15:38.879
Here it is.

00:15:38.879 --> 00:15:40.079
Let's continue further.

00:15:40.079 --> 00:15:42.579
GT, we need GT.

00:15:42.579 --> 00:15:49.910
And then at the next step, we need to find
something starting from TT.

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:50.910
But look.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:56.980
We don't have any reads starting from TT,
which means that we failed.

00:15:56.980 --> 00:16:06.579
So we need to develop something that is a
better way of solving this assembly problem.

00:16:06.579 --> 00:16:12.250
So let me describe how biologists have been
solving this problem for a long time.

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:19.829
In fact, I'm describing an approach that was
used for assembling the human genome.

00:16:19.829 --> 00:16:23.860
Let's represent every k-mer as a node.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:31.620
And let's connect nodes corresponding to the
genome by arrows.

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:34.819
I call this arrows.

00:16:34.819 --> 00:16:37.350
[? Algorithmists ?] call these arrows [? edges.

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:45.480
?] And as a result, the sequence of this node
will basically read the genome.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:50.560
But how do they know?

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:57.370
So we've done a little bit of chicken, because
we constructed this graph, assuming we know

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:58.920
the genome.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:05.339
But can we construct this genome path from
the first node to the last node without knowing

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:07.449
the genome, only from its composition?

00:17:07.449 --> 00:17:11.180
At first glance, it looks scarce.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:18.830
We simply need to connect k-mer 1 with k-mer
2, if suffix of the first k-mer equal to the

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:22.810
prefix of the second k-mer, like shown here.

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:29.890
And in this case, they will construct exactly
the same path, right?

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:31.780
So that's what I've done.

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:38.660
But if you follow these rules that are outlined
at the bottom of this slide, then you have

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:45.320
an obligation to connect every two k-mers
such that suffix of the first one equal to

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:51.480
the prefix of the second one, which means
that you have to connect AAT with ATG.

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:56.580
And you have to connect AAT with [INAUDIBLE],
then continue in this way.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:18:00.400
This is all the connections that you have
to make.

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:05.720
And that's why the problem becomes more complex.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.040
And can we still find the genome path in this
graph?

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:09.040
Of course.

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:10.940
It's still this horizontal path.

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:17.830
But we forgot that we do not know the order
[? of ?] correct number of nodes in this graph.

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:23.980
And if [? we ?] order nodes on this graph
in the same way as we do in the phone book,

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:26.610
then the graph looks like this.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:28.980
It looks like a very complex maze.

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:33.920
And it's absolutely not clear how would we
assemble genomes from this graph.

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:39.420
But clearly, we need to travel in this graph,
starting [? again ?] with TAA, continuing

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:40.420
further.

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:48.070
Continuing further, please note that there
are multiplied choices [? at ?] many steps.

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:53.780
So we continue, continue, continue, continue.

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:57.810
And well, what are we trying to do?

