all edges of the graph and solve the problem.

00:37:22.299 --> 00:37:30.950
But a less intelligent ant would go and would
start working, and probably would start somewhere.

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:34.359
So the ant walk randomly.

00:37:34.359 --> 00:37:37.960
And in this case, it actually stuck in the
node.

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:45.869
There is no egress out of this node, and the
ant has not completed that Eulerian walk yet.

00:37:45.869 --> 00:37:48.400
Well, what should we do?

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:51.660
His idea is that Euler came [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:54.250
You know what?

00:37:54.250 --> 00:37:56.299
Ant actually did a good job.

00:37:56.299 --> 00:38:00.319
He just started at the wrong node.

00:38:00.319 --> 00:38:03.730
Maybe we can ask ant to start at a different
node.

00:38:03.730 --> 00:38:06.329
Well, what node would it be?

00:38:06.329 --> 00:38:12.489
Well, if you look at this green cycle, there
are some nodes on this green cycle [? at this

00:38:12.489 --> 00:38:16.880
?] still unexplored address.

00:38:16.880 --> 00:38:19.869
And why shouldn't we do this?

00:38:19.869 --> 00:38:29.390
Or why shouldn't we start at the node to these
unexplored edges and still continue walking

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:32.910
along the green cycle, but starting from this
node.

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:38.900
The point is that, when we return back, there
will be an edge to continue our walk.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:39.970
Let's do this.

00:38:39.970 --> 00:38:44.170
This is first edge, second, third, fourth.

00:38:44.170 --> 00:38:46.910
But now there is a possibility to move further,
right?

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:51.500
We move further, move further, move further,
move further.

00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:53.539
And we're stuck again.

00:38:53.539 --> 00:38:56.089
Well, what should we do?

00:38:56.089 --> 00:39:02.140
Well, now you've probably figured out that
we need to continue walking along this cycle

00:39:02.140 --> 00:39:06.000
that ant just completed, but starting from
a different node.

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:07.990
Well, what node should we select?

00:39:07.990 --> 00:39:15.710
Once again, node will be a still unexplored
address, or which will be this node.

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:17.290
So what do you do?

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:24.980
We once again, we utilize the previous job
that the ant did and walk along the previous

00:39:24.980 --> 00:39:25.980
cycle.

00:39:25.980 --> 00:39:29.220
So that's what we do.

00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:32.080
Now we return back to the right node.

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:33.559
But we can't continue walking.

00:39:33.559 --> 00:39:36.500
So ant continue walking, continue walking,
continue walking.

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:42.130
And finally, ant completed the Eulerian cycle.

00:39:42.130 --> 00:39:51.900
The beauty of this proof is that it immediately
implies a very efficient algorithm for solving

00:39:51.900 --> 00:40:00.619
the Eulerian cycle problem, because what I
just described

00:40:00.619 --> 00:40:07.590
was very essentially algorithmic instruction
to the ant or to your computer on how to find

00:40:07.590 --> 00:40:09.339
such cycles.

00:40:09.339 --> 00:40:12.650
And the algorithm is very efficient.

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:17.269
Now, when you formulate and when you [? broke
?] collaboratively, you [? lose ?] your computational

00:40:17.269 --> 00:40:21.680
[INAUDIBLE] a good program for them.

00:40:21.680 --> 00:40:27.050
But it's also very important that your problems
adequately model reality.

00:40:27.050 --> 00:40:31.900
So problems that I describe doesn't model
reality of assembly.

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:37.660
And that's why there are 1,000s of papers
on this topic, and many, many groups working

00:40:37.660 --> 00:40:41.069
