00:40:41.069 --> 00:40:45.509
So in [INAUDIBLE], they made sound ridiculously
unrealistic assumption.

00:40:45.509 --> 00:40:51.450
For example, they assume that there is perfect
coverage of genome by reads, which means every

00:40:51.450 --> 00:40:53.920
k-mer from the genome is represented by reads.

00:40:53.920 --> 00:40:56.180
This is wrong, of course, in real biological
problem.

00:40:56.180 --> 00:40:59.590
They assume that reads are error-free, which
is wrong.

00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:02.530
They assume that multiplicities of k-mers
are known.

00:41:02.530 --> 00:41:04.519
Many, many wrong assumptions are made here.

00:41:04.519 --> 00:41:08.119
In fact, in reality, we have imperfect coverage.

00:41:08.119 --> 00:41:09.470
Reads are errorprone.

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:11.720
Multiplicities of k-mers are unknown.

00:41:11.720 --> 00:41:17.940
And computer scientists and algorithmists
know how to deal with this assumption.

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:23.670
Also, of course, modern [? assemblers ?] is
one of the most sophisticated pieces of hardware

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:27.760
today-- of software in modern biology.

00:41:27.760 --> 00:41:34.859
And for example, in the case of imperfect
read coverage, their assumption [? is ?] counter-intuitive.

00:41:34.859 --> 00:41:35.859
They do.

00:41:35.859 --> 00:41:40.990
They actually break every reads into shorter
k-mers to do assembly.

00:41:40.990 --> 00:41:46.060
For example, if you generated 250-nucleotide
[INAUDIBLE] reads, computer scientists will

00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:53.040
most likely break them into, let's say, 55-nucleotide
reads to ensure better coverage by short k-mers.

00:41:53.040 --> 00:41:57.720
Another things, what do they do with errors
in reads?

00:41:57.720 --> 00:42:04.940
Well, in this case, if you have erroneous
reads, then your perfect genomic path that

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:09.609
