00:56:58.460 --> 00:57:10.960
So most of [INAUDIBLE] in their assembly is
concentrated in places like MIT, Beijing Genome

00:57:10.960 --> 00:57:22.019
Institute, in British Columbia, Genome Center,
and other places, and my own lab.

00:57:22.019 --> 00:57:27.400
So I would say most of efforts in this are
actually happening in academia, rather than

00:57:27.400 --> 00:57:28.400
in company.

00:57:28.400 --> 00:57:29.400
Interesting.

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:33.269
Has anybody who's working in the field, have
they attempted to play with algorithms which

00:57:33.269 --> 00:57:41.599
start with longer k-mers, to minimize possibly
the number of edges and nodes, and then simplify

00:57:41.599 --> 00:57:47.240
things, and try to resolve things that were
not possible under the smaller size?

00:57:47.240 --> 00:57:48.720
Does that make sense?

00:57:48.720 --> 00:57:49.720
Yes.

00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:51.260
People definitely want to play with longer
k-mer.

00:57:51.260 --> 00:57:58.550
In fact, the [INAUDIBLE] people at University
of Hong Kong, who develop [? IDBA, ?] popular

00:57:58.550 --> 00:58:05.920
[? IDBA ?] Assembler, [INAUDIBLE] first realize
how to incorporate longer and longer k-mers

00:58:05.920 --> 00:58:07.950
in the de Bruijn graph.

00:58:07.950 --> 00:58:16.380
And they developed the concept of iterative
assembly, [? where ?] when kmer lens is not

00:58:16.380 --> 00:58:21.450
fixed, like in the setups that I presented,
but is [? changing.

00:58:21.450 --> 00:58:25.849
?] So and that allows them to incorporate
longer k-mers in the framework.

00:58:25.849 --> 00:58:31.190
Well, one last question is I wasn't quite
clear on how you identified the bubbles that

00:58:31.190 --> 00:58:33.910
you mentioned in some of your de Bruijn graphs.

00:58:33.910 --> 00:58:38.239
I'm sure that that's kind of a laborious process,
to identify them, and probably an even

00:58:38.239 --> 00:58:43.250
more laborious process to decide which ones
are real and which ones are, in fact, bubbles

00:58:43.250 --> 00:58:46.079
and can be popped, if you will.

00:58:46.079 --> 00:58:51.599
Just a little summary of how that process
happens and how long it takes?

00:58:51.599 --> 00:59:01.170
So bubble are simply represented as a short
cycle in the graph, right?

00:59:01.170 --> 00:59:03.369
So that's how it looks like.

00:59:03.369 --> 00:59:10.660
Because every error in a read creates relatively
a short-- they create basically alternative

00:59:10.660 --> 00:59:15.040
paths between two nodes.

00:59:15.040 --> 00:59:17.650
Now, finding them is not a problem.

00:59:17.650 --> 00:59:22.200
It's actually difficult not to notice them,
because there are so many them.

00:59:22.200 --> 00:59:24.000
Every read usually has an error.

00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:28.519
So imagine you have millions of reads mean
millions [? of model.

00:59:28.519 --> 00:59:35.829
?] The question is, how to efficiently get
rid of them and how to remove them from the

00:59:35.829 --> 00:59:39.609
graph without destroying the correct errors.

00:59:39.609 --> 00:59:44.569
That would take me another hour to cover.

00:59:44.569 --> 00:59:49.710
[LAUGHS] Well, maybe we'll have to find another
hour for you to explain that process.

00:59:49.710 --> 00:59:53.349
It sounds like that's pretty laborious.

00:59:53.349 --> 00:59:55.259
But at any rate.

00:59:55.259 --> 00:59:58.529
Well, listen, everyone, we've reached the
top of the hour here.

